# Prefer SSH git remotes for push

GitHub HTTPS pushes via the Cursor/OAuth app **cannot update** `.github/workflows/*` (missing `workflow` scope) and are weaker for force-with-lease / protected-branch operations. SSH uses the user's key and has full repo privileges.

## Before push — ensure SSH origin

```bash
# ✅ GOOD
git remote set-url origin git@github.com:martinhickson/IcedTea-Web-1.git
git remote -v   # fetch/push both git@github.com:...
git push origin HEAD
```

```bash
# ❌ BAD — HTTPS OAuth often fails on workflow files
git remote set-url origin https://github.com/martinhickson/IcedTea-Web-1.git
git push origin HEAD
# rejected: refusing to allow an OAuth App to create or update workflow ... without workflow scope
```

## Rules of thumb

1. Prefer `git@github.com:martinhickson/IcedTea-Web-1.git` for **push** (and usually fetch too).
2. If `git remote -v` shows `https://github.com/...`, switch to SSH **before** pushing — especially when the commit touches `.github/workflows/`.
3. Do not fall back to HTTPS after an OAuth rejection; fix the remote to SSH and retry.
4. `gh` API/CLI may still use HTTPS auth; that is fine for releases/PRs. **Git push/fetch of refs** should use SSH.
